From: DashBerry <zero/README.md>
Subject: [PATCH] uvc: do not dereference the frame format we failed to find

uvc_get_frame_format() returns -1 and leaves the caller's pointer untouched
when no table entry matches. Its result was discarded:

    struct uvc_frame_format * frame_format;
    uvc_get_frame_format(&frame_format, iformat, iframe);
    uvc_dump_frame_format(frame_format, "FRAME");     /* uninitialised */

so a host asking for a (format, frame) pair the table does not hold reads an
uninitialised stack pointer one line later.

This is not a corner case on this image. uvc_fill_streaming_control() runs at
startup with (0, 0), and both indices are clamped against ranges that are -1
when the table is empty - so a discovery that recorded nothing arrives here
directly.

What makes it expensive is that it is invisible from the host. f_uvc answers
standard requests out of the kernel, so the device enumerates perfectly, with
correct H.264 descriptors, whether or not uvc-gadget parsed anything: the
gadget's descriptors and uvc-gadget's table are two independent readings of
the same configfs tree. Only the first CLASS-specific request reaches this
code. When it dies there, ep0 stops being serviced for the entire gadget, and
the symptom is the host timing out on control transfers and the login console
hanging along with the camera - which reads like a USB or driver fault rather
than a userspace crash.

Measured: gst-launch failing after 10.5 s (two 5 s control-transfer timeouts)
with "Buffer pool activation failed", ttyACM0 hung afterwards, and identical
behaviour at 1280x720 and 1640x922 - i.e. not resolution or bandwidth.

Answer badly rather than not at all. A missing entry now names itself, prints
the whole table, and falls back to the first defined entry so the gadget keeps
servicing ep0; an EMPTY table says so in as many words and returns a zeroed
control. The host can reject a wrong answer. It cannot do anything with
silence, and silence also costs the console and the control port.
---
--- a/uvc-gadget.c
+++ b/uvc-gadget.c
@@ -1425,8 +1425,55 @@
         iframe = clamp(iframe, format_frame_first, format_frame_last);
     }
 
-    struct uvc_frame_format * frame_format;
-    uvc_get_frame_format(&frame_format, iformat, iframe);
+    /* DashBerry: uvc_get_frame_format() returns -1 and leaves the pointer
+     * ALONE when nothing matches, and its result was discarded - so a host
+     * asking for a (format, frame) the table does not hold dereferenced an
+     * uninitialised stack pointer one line later.
+     *
+     * That is not a corner case here. This function runs at startup with
+     * (0, 0), and both indices are clamped against ranges that are -1 when the
+     * table is empty, so a discovery that recorded nothing lands straight on
+     * it. And the failure is invisible from the host: f_uvc answers standard
+     * requests from the kernel, so the device still ENUMERATES perfectly with
+     * correct H.264 descriptors - it is only the first class-specific request
+     * that reaches this code. When it dies, ep0 stops being serviced for the
+     * whole gadget, so the symptom is the host timing out (2 x 5 s) and the
+     * CONSOLE hanging with the camera, which reads like a USB fault rather
+     * than a userspace crash.
+     *
+     * Answer badly rather than not at all: a zeroed control is a wrong answer
+     * the host can reject, whereas silence costs the console and the control
+     * port too.
+     */
+    struct uvc_frame_format * frame_format = NULL;
+    int have;
+
+    if (uvc_get_frame_format(&frame_format, iformat, iframe) < 0 || frame_format == NULL) {
+        printf("UVC: NO TABLE ENTRY for format %d frame %d - the host asked for "
+               "something discovery never recorded.\n", iformat, iframe);
+        printf("UVC: format range [%d, %d], frame range [%d, %d]; the table holds:\n",
+               format_first, format_last, frame_first, frame_last);
+
+        frame_format = NULL;
+        for (have = 0; have <= last_format_index; have++) {
+            if (uvc_frame_format[have].defined) {
+                uvc_dump_frame_format(&uvc_frame_format[have], "UVC:   have");
+                if (frame_format == NULL) {
+                    frame_format = &uvc_frame_format[have];
+                }
+            }
+        }
+
+        if (frame_format == NULL) {
+            printf("UVC:   (nothing - the format table is EMPTY)\n");
+            printf("UVC: configfs discovery found no format, so this gadget cannot "
+                   "answer any streaming request. Read the CONFIGFS lines above.\n");
+            memset(ctrl, 0, sizeof * ctrl);
+            return;
+        }
+        printf("UVC: falling back to the first entry above so the gadget keeps "
+               "answering; expect the host to reject it.\n");
+    }
 
     uvc_dump_frame_format(frame_format, "FRAME");
 
